% quit ends the run it is executed in, whatever device that run is on.
% Run against a live device (-d <dev>) by run-device-quit-test.sh: prints
% SUCCESS iff nothing after the quit runs.
%
% PLRM 8.2 gives quit one job, to terminate operation of the interpreter.
% Nothing after it in the file it was read from runs, and neither does
% anything the frames beneath it had left to do.
%
% The device is what made this vary. Ending a run tears the device down,
% and a device whose Destroy is a PostScript procedure is torn down by
% re-entering the interpreter to run it. A re-entry with no stop of its
% own carries on past the procedure into the frames the abandoned program
% left behind, and runs the rest of the file. A device whose Destroy is
% an operator is called directly, re-enters nothing, and so never showed
% it.
%
% The verdict is printed before the quit because there is no after: a
% line printed here at all is the failure, and the wrapper judges a run
% that printed one as failed however it ended.

/ender {
    (SUCCESS) =
    quit
    (FAILURE: the procedure continued past quit) =
} def

ender

(FAILURE: the run continued past quit) =
